<Base title="About" description="What nuclear.academy is, who writes it, and what it is not.">
  <div class="kicker">About</div>
  <h1>What this site is, and is not</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>nuclear.academy is an open course of study in nuclear security: how the world keeps nuclear material where it belongs, detects activity outside the bounds of appropriate use, and responds when something goes wrong. It is written for readers who follow the subject and want the mechanism rather than the headline — the technically or policy-engaged public first, and students and practitioners alongside them.</p>
    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>Howard L. Hall is Professor of Nuclear Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, and founder of its Institute for Nuclear Security. He came to the university in 2009 after two decades at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory.</p>
    <h2>How it is organized</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Foundations</strong> — five short units for readers in grades 8–12 or anyone without a physics course: atoms, isotopes and the chart of the nuclides, decay, nuclear reactions, and the math.</li>
      <li><strong>Modules</strong> — the course of study: why nuclear security matters; the fuel cycle; how safeguards work; nuclear forensics; fusion. Each has stated objectives, numbered sources, and questions to explore.</li>
      <li><strong>Tools</strong> — a chart-of-the-nuclides explorer and a decay-chain calculator, both computed in your browser from the evaluated nuclear data.</li>
      <li><strong>Atlas</strong> — the civil fuel cycle on a map, with each facility linked to the public record it came from.</li>
      <li><strong>Open questions</strong> — one page per live policy argument, each side stated as its proponents make it.</li>
      <li><strong>Situation</strong> — dated pages carrying the figures that change, re-checked on the date shown.</li>
      <li><strong>Glossary and sources</strong> — every term the modules use, and every document they cite.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>For teachers</h2>
    <p>The Foundations units are written at a nominal tenth-grade level and end with tasks on the site's tools; a teacher's note mapping each unit to standards is in preparation. Modules carry learning objectives that can be used directly. Everything is licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">CC BY-NC-ND 4.0</a>: you may copy and share it for teaching with attribution, but not sell it or alter it.</p>
    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>It is not news, not advocacy, and not a guide to anything sensitive. Everything here is built from the open literature — IAEA guidance, U.S. regulations, peer-reviewed work, public agency documents — and says where it came from. Durable modules are kept apart from dated situation pages. Nothing you do here is tracked.</p>
    <p class="meta"><em>Affiliation statement and acknowledgements to be settled by the editor before first release.</em></p>
  </div>
